Instead of scanning in two directions, this version processes children from the lowest rating to the highest. A child only needs to be higher than a neighboring child with a lower rating, so that neighbor has already been processed when the current child is considered.

Equal ratings do not create a requirement, so their order in the sorted list does not matter. For `[1, 0, 2]`, the child with rating `0` stays at 1, then the two higher-rated children receive 2 candies each.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int candy(int[] ratings) {
        int n = ratings.length;
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[][] order = new int[n][2];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            order[i][0] = ratings[i];
            order[i][1] = i;
        }

        Arrays.sort(order, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));

        int[] candies = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(candies, 1);

        for (int[] item : order) {
            int index = item[1];
            int needed = 1;

            if (index > 0 && ratings[index - 1] < ratings[index]) {
                needed = Math.max(needed, candies[index - 1] + 1);
            }
            if (index + 1 < n && ratings[index + 1] < ratings[index]) {
                needed = Math.max(needed, candies[index + 1] + 1);
            }

            candies[index] = needed;
        }

        int total = 0;
        for (int count : candies) {
            total += count;
        }
        return total;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the sorted index pairs and candy counts.

This is slower than a linear scan, but the dependency idea is quite direct.

This is the straightforward version. Give everyone one candy, then keep scanning the line. Whenever a higher-rated child does not have more candy than a lower-rated neighbor, increase the higher child's count. The loop stops when a full scan makes no change.

It is not the fastest method, but the rule in the code is easy to match with the problem statement. Equal ratings are ignored because neither child must receive more than the other.

For the edge case `[5]`, the initial list is `[1]` and the answer is 1. For `[2, 2, 2]`, no update is made and the answer is 3.

```python
class Solution:
    def candy(self, ratings):
        n = len(ratings)
        candies = [1] * n
        changed = True

        while changed:
            changed = False
            for i in range(n):
                if i > 0:
                    if ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1] and candies[i] <= candies[i - 1]:
                        candies[i] = candies[i - 1] + 1
                        changed = True
                    elif ratings[i] < ratings[i - 1] and candies[i] >= candies[i - 1]:
                        candies[i - 1] = candies[i] + 1
                        changed = True

                if i + 1 < n:
                    if ratings[i] > ratings[i + 1] and candies[i] <= candies[i + 1]:
                        candies[i] = candies[i + 1] + 1
                        changed = True
                    elif ratings[i] < ratings[i + 1] and candies[i] >= candies[i + 1]:
                        candies[i + 1] = candies[i] + 1
                        changed = True

        return sum(candies)
```

The worst-case time complexity is `O(n^2)` because several full scans may be needed. Space complexity is `O(n)`. The ratings list is not modified.

This keeps only a few counters instead of storing candy counts for every child. `up` counts a rising run, `down` counts a falling run, and `peak` remembers the height of the last rise. Equal ratings reset the runs because they place no extra condition on either child.

One edge case is a completely decreasing list such as `[4, 3, 2, 1]`. There is no rising peak, so the falling count adds the needed `3 + 2 + 1` extra candies to the four base candies.

```go
func candy(ratings []int) int {
    if len(ratings) == 0 {
        return 0
    }

    total := 1
    up := 0
    down := 0
    peak := 0

    for i := 1; i < len(ratings); i++ {
        if ratings[i] > ratings[i-1] {
            up++
            down = 0
            peak = up
            total += up + 1
        } else if ratings[i] == ratings[i-1] {
            up = 0
            down = 0
            peak = 0
            total++
        } else {
            up = 0
            down++
            total += down + 1
            if peak >= down {
                total--
            }
        }
    }

    return total
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The input slice is only read. No candy array is created.

I kept an array of candy counts and started every value at 1. The first pass handles children who are higher rated than the child on the left. The second pass handles the right side and uses `max` so a value from the first pass is not lost.

This is easy to check on a peak such as `[1, 2, 3, 2, 1]`, which becomes `[1, 2, 3, 2, 1]`. The input ratings array is not changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int candy(vector<int>& ratings) {
        int n = ratings.size();
        vector<int> candies(n, 1);

        for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
            if (ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]) {
                candies[i] = candies[i - 1] + 1;
            }
        }

        for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; --i) {
            if (ratings[i] > ratings[i + 1]) {
                candies[i] = max(candies[i], candies[i + 1] + 1);
            }
        }

        int total = 0;
        for (int value : candies) {
            total += value;
        }
        return total;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the candy array.

## Intuition
Two-pass greedy approach. 

## Approach
The rule is:

Every child must get at least 1 candy.
If a child has a higher rating than an adjacent child, they must get more candies.

The tricky part is that a child can have to satisfy both left and right neighbors.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    int candy(vector<int>& ratings) {
       int n=ratings.size();
       vector<int> count(n,1);
       for(int i=1;i<n;i++){
        if(ratings[i]>ratings[i-1]){
            count[i]=max(count[i],count[i-1]+1);
        }
       }
       for(int i=n-2;i>=0;i--){
        if(ratings[i]>ratings[i+1]){
            count[i]=max(count[i],count[i+1]+1);
        }
       }
       int result=0;
       for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
        result+=count[i];
       }
       return result;
    }
};
```

